Chain: Change

Purpose:
  The stage sequence for cases where already-completed work must be
  made architecturally or behaviorally different — the "a later prompt
  revealed this should have been done differently" scenario. AN/AR/DB/
  BE/API/FE/MD/QA/DO/VR are not redefined here — see templates/
  feature.sdd -> Stages; IMPACT is not redefined either — see
  templates/bugfix.sdd -> Stages (same name, different meaning in this
  context, explained below).

Sequence:
  AN -> IMPACT -> AR -> DB -> BE -> API -> FE -> MD -> QA -> DO -> VR

Stages:

  AN, AR, DB, BE, API, FE, MD, QA, DO, VR -> see templates/feature.sdd
    -> Stages / ../stages/*.sdd (same stages, same meaning)

  IMPACT -> see templates/bugfix.sdd -> Stages -> IMPACT (same name,
    different meaning here — see Rules [CH2] below)

Rules:
  [CH1] This chain is effectively templates/feature.sdd's own Sequence,
        with one IMPACT stage inserted right after AN (compare Sequence
        above with templates/feature.sdd -> Sequence).
  [CH2] IMPACT here carries a different meaning than in templates/
        bugfix.sdd: it does not determine a bug's impact surface, it
        determines which already-completed stages' outputs the change
        invalidates (see chains.sdd -> Rules [C15], reopen-on-
        invalidation).
  [CH3] Every completed stage IMPACT identifies MUST be reopened (state
        -> active again, see PROJECT.sdd -> State işarələri) — it MUST
        NOT be silently passed over (see chains.sdd -> Rules [C7]).

State: +

Note:
  (prompt/new/33.md, STEP 11) Supersedes ../change.chain (STEP 5),
  deleted in this same pass (preserved in git history).

  Difference from templates/feature.sdd: change.sdd operates over
  already-existing, completed work and may conditionally invalidate
  prior results; feature.sdd starts from zero and has no need for this
  reopen-invalidation logic.

  Content translated from the original Azerbaijani (old change.chain)
  into English, per the standing "apply, don't copy" rule.
